The technical implementation requires two key ingredients. First, we use automatic differentiation in PyTorch (Paszke et al., 2017) to calculate the gradients of the interaction potentials to obtain the forces. Second, we want to avoid imposing assumptions on the potentials from their parametric form so we use non-parametric potentials in the form of neural networks.
Automatic Differentiation of Potentials. With traditional methods, the dimensionality of the parameter space is crucial for numerical optimizers and gradient approximation. In addition, second-order optimizers like L-BFGS (Byrd et al., 1995; Broyden, 1970; Fletcher, 1970; Goldfarb, 1970; Shanno, 1970) need to store an approximation of the function curvature. Our proposed method provides exact gradients without costly numerical gradient estimation and uses first-order optimization methods – Stochastic Gradient Descent (Bottou, 2010) – that are suitable for the stochastic setting. This is faster and more stable than numeric differentiation and more flexible than hand-coded derivatives of particular potentials.
